Hello 3D ADEPT team,

an editorial lead on a design decision rather than a feature list.

Solidon3D reads downloaded 3D models and makes their features editable without
CAD knowledge. Two boundaries in the product may be worth a paragraph.

The first is bounded recognition. The software shows only what a printer can
actually produce: features below the resolution of the process are not listed.
On a downloaded hose holder this removes several hundred entries from the
object tree that were measurement noise. The point is not the number — it is
that a tool of this kind has to decide what it does not say, or the result is
an inventory nobody can use.

The second is bounded AI. There is an assistant, it can drive the same
operations the menus do, and it is entirely optional: every function of the
software works without it, it can be switched off, and it can run locally on
the user's own GPU rather than in a service. Nothing is sent anywhere by
default; after a single activation the application runs fully offline, with no
account and no telemetry.

The release itself, briefly: with 0.5.0 an imported mesh becomes an exact
body. Holes, slots, countersinks, bosses, beads, grooves and threads stay
exact through moving, duplicating, rotating and removing, and STEP round-trips
cleanly. The options that used to switch between the mesh and the exact kernel
have been removed. A honeycomb pattern or knurl is now one feature with pitch,
cell width and depth instead of hundreds of faces. Resin printers are
supported. Recognition is several times faster.

70 user-visible changes in this release; 896 since the first 0.1 demo.
Interface and manual in six languages.

Solidon3D is a desktop application for Windows, macOS and Linux. One-time
purchase, no subscription, no account: after a single activation it runs fully
offline. Interface and manual are available in six languages, and there is a
demo.

I am happy to provide a review licence, print-resolution images or answers to
any question directly.

